<h2>A staples trade, not a discretionary one</h2>
<p>Iraq's basmati demand is different in character from the Gulf tourism-and-retail-adjacent markets: this is food-security-scale staple trade, and it shows in the numbers — roughly USD 862 million in 2024-25, India's second-largest single-country basmati market after Saudi Arabia. Rice here is eaten in volume across the population, and Iraqi buying patterns favour the same long-grain, well-aged 1121 that dominates Gulf demand generally.</p>

<h2>Government tenders and private trade run side by side</h2>
<p>A meaningful share of Iraqi rice purchasing happens through government or state-trading-linked tenders, which is worth knowing if you're new to this market — it means large-volume public-sector demand exists alongside, not instead of, ordinary commercial trade. If you're a private buyer, none of that changes your process: you order against your specification, we quote and ship on standard commercial terms, entirely separate from any tender mechanism.</p>

<h2>Dry cargo simplifies the 8–14 day transit</h2>
<p>Basmati's advantage on this route is that it doesn't need what banana or other fresh produce shipped to Iraq needs — an unbroken reefer chain. Rice is dry cargo with a genuinely long shelf life, so the 8–14 day transit to Umm Qasr is a straightforward scheduling variable rather than a cold-chain risk to manage. If you're sourcing both rice and perishables from us for the Iraqi market, expect a much simpler logistics conversation on the rice side.</p>

<h2>Build documentation lead time into your schedule</h2>
<p>Certificates of origin bound for Iraq typically need chamber of commerce attestation, and sometimes consular legalisation on top of that — steps that add real processing days compared with the more streamlined documentation common in Gulf markets like the UAE. Start this paperwork as soon as the order is confirmed rather than treating it as a same-week formality before the vessel sails.</p>

